+# case_insensitive_autoescape
+
+## Why
+Default Jinja autoescaping treats supported template suffixes as case-sensitive, so names such as `invoice.HTML` do not receive the same escaping as `invoice.html`.
+
+## What
+- Recognize the existing default autoescaped suffixes regardless of filename case.
+- Preserve the results for unsupported suffixes and `None`.
+- Non-goal: add extensions or alter custom autoescape callables.
+
+## How
+Normalize the filename's case immediately before the existing suffix comparison.
+
+## Impact
+- `Flask.create_jinja_environment` installs this selector as Jinja's default autoescape callable.
+- The selector has no alternate framework entry point, stored data, or permission behavior; applications may override it.
+- This is a compatible expansion of the existing supported filename behavior.
+
+## Acceptance
+- [x] Every existing supported extension enables autoescaping with uppercase or mixed-case letters — test: `test_default_autoescape_extensions`
+- [x] Unsupported filenames still disable autoescaping and `None` still enables it — test: `test_default_autoescape_extensions`
+- [x] The public test suite passes — check: `PYTHONPATH=src:.bench-pydeps python3 -m pytest tests -q`
+
+## Open questions
+None.
+
+## Decisions
+- templating: the default selector normalizes only filename case; the existing supported extension list remains the compatibility boundary.
